From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 30 Aug 2026 12:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Bind every V4L2 node into the sandbox, not just video0-2

bindV4l() hardcodes /dev/video0, /dev/video1 and /dev/video2, with a
comment calling itself a stop-gap. V4L2 node numbers are handed out in
probe order, so any SoC whose camera registers several nodes before the
video codec puts that codec above video2, and it is then absent from the
web process' /dev entirely.

On a Pixel 2 XL (msm8998, mainline 7.2) qcom-camss takes video0 through
video5 and Venus registers the decoder as /dev/video7. Inside the sandbox
only video0-2 exist, so gstreamer's v4l2 decoder elements cannot open the
device, GStreamerRegistryScanner drops them, and playback silently falls
back to avdec_h264. Measured on a 720p H.264 clip: 154% of a CPU core
sandboxed against 41% with WEBKIT_DISABLE_SANDBOX_THIS_IS_DANGEROUS=1,
where the log shows v4l2h264dec being created instead. Nothing is logged
when this happens -- the decoder simply is not among the candidates -- so
it presents as "hardware decoding does not work in the browser" while the
identical pipeline works in gst-launch outside the sandbox.

Enumerate /dev and bind each video* and media* node through the existing
bindIfExists() helper rather than naming three of them. This exposes no
class of device the function was not already binding; it stops the set
from depending on enumeration order.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 .../Launcher/glib/BubblewrapLauncher.cpp      | 25 ++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

--- a/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/Launcher/glib/BubblewrapLauncher.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/Launcher/glib/BubblewrapLauncher.cpp
@@ -456,12 +456,29 @@
 {
     args.appendVector(Vector<CString>({
         "--dev-bind-try", "/dev/v4l", "/dev/v4l",
-        // Not pretty but a stop-gap for pipewire anyway.
-        "--dev-bind-try", "/dev/video0", "/dev/video0",
-        "--dev-bind-try", "/dev/video1", "/dev/video1",
-        "--dev-bind-try", "/dev/video2", "/dev/video2",
-        "--dev-bind-try", "/dev/media0", "/dev/media0",
     }));
+
+    // Bind every V4L2 node instead of a hardcoded video0-2 range. Node numbers
+    // are handed out in probe order, so on an SoC whose camera registers
+    // several nodes first the video codec lands well above video2 and is then
+    // invisible inside the sandbox. On a Pixel 2 XL (msm8998) camss takes
+    // video0-video5 and the Venus decoder is /dev/video7, so the v4l2 decoder
+    // elements were unusable in the web process and playback silently fell
+    // back to software -- 154% of a CPU core for 720p H.264 where the hardware
+    // path costs 41% -- while the very same decode worked outside the sandbox.
+    GDir* dir = g_dir_open("/dev", 0, nullptr);
+    if (!dir)
+        return;
+
+    while (const char* name = g_dir_read_name(dir)) {
+        if (!g_str_has_prefix(name, "video") && !g_str_has_prefix(name, "media"))
+            continue;
+
+        GUniquePtr<char> path(g_build_filename("/dev", name, nullptr));
+        bindIfExists(args, path.get(), BindFlags::Device);
+    }
+
+    g_dir_close(dir);
 }
 
 static bool enableDebugPermissions()
